            "text": "An efficient implementation using double-precision matrix multiplication via BLAS. By converting to `float64` and computing pairwise squared Euclidean distances via the Gram matrix $\\|x_i - x_j\\|^2 = \\|x_i\\|^2 + \\|x_j\\|^2 - 2\\langle x_i, x_j \\rangle$, the computation completes in a few seconds while avoiding memory explosion and floating-point cancellation.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Load input array and promote to float64 for numerical precision\n    X = np.load(\"vectors.npy\").astype(np.float64)\n\n    # Compute row-wise squared Euclidean norms: shape (8000,)\n    sq_norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    # Compute Gram matrix G = X @ X.T: shape (8000, 8000)\n    # ||x_i - x_j||^2 = ||x_i||^2 + ||x_j||^2 - 2 * <x_i, x_j>\n    D2 = X @ X.T\n    D2 *= -2.0\n    D2 += sq_norms[:, None]\n    D2 += sq_norms[None, :]\n\n    # Clamp numerical inaccuracies: exact zeros on the diagonal and non-negative values\n    np.fill_diagonal(D2, 0.0)\n    np.maximum(D2, 0.0, out=D2)\n\n    # Euclidean distance matrix\n    np.sqrt(D2, out=D2)\n\n    total = float(np.sum(D2))\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
